Ladybird keeps auto-sized native form controls at their natural size
when anchor-center aligns them, even with both insets definite. The
sizing decision treats only normal and stretch as stretch alignment,
while anchor positioning runs separately.

Blink’s auto_size_behavior lambda selects fit-content sizing for
anchor-center in OutOfFlowLayoutPart::TryCalculateOffset(). Gecko
makes that same alignment distinction through its shouldStretch
lambda in nsIFrame::ComputeAbsolutePosAutoSize().

WebKit uses PositionedLayoutConstraints::alignmentAppliesStretch(),
which returns false for anchor-center. Its width-sizing function,
RenderBox::computeOutOfFlowPositionedLogicalWidthUsing(), therefore
selects fit-content sizing. Ladybird matches their sizing behavior,
while keeping anchor placement on its own separate path.

Ladybird lets anchor-center resolve to center for self-alignment when
anchor lookup supplies no anchor placement. A valid anchor placement
still takes precedence over the ordinary self-alignment fallback.

Blink uses the same equal inset bias for center and anchor-center in
GetAlignmentInsetBias(). ComputeAnchorCenterPosition() supplies no
anchor offsets when AnchorCenterOffset() is absent, leaving that bias
to center the box. Ladybird matches this missing-anchor behavior.

WebKit’s PositionedLayoutConstraints::resolveAlignmentValue() returns
Center when the alignment is AnchorCenter and m_defaultAnchorBox is
absent. Gecko’s CSSAlignUtils::AlignJustifySelf() uses the same center
calculation for CENTER and ANCHOR_CENTER when no AnchorAlignInfo is
supplied. Both match Ladybird’s fallback on the inline and block axes.
